00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,302
with those who pose a threat to the state

5
00:00:03,402 --> 00:00:06,539
and is therefore a threat to him.

6
00:00:10,209 --> 00:00:15,515
NARRATOR: At just 28 years old, Kim Jong-Un became the leader of his country,

7
00:00:15,615 --> 00:00:19,118
the third man to rule North Korea.

8
00:00:19,218 --> 00:00:24,357
He took over his father's nuclear weapons program and supercharged it.

9
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:32,565
MADDEN: North Korea's Ballistic Missile and Nuclear Weapons Achievements

10
00:00:32,665 --> 00:00:36,970
are the result of Kim Jong-Un's characteristics as a leader.

11
00:00:37,070 --> 00:00:41,808
He far surpassed the lifetime achievements of his father and grandfather.

12
00:00:46,112 --> 00:00:52,318
JERROLD: You can't understand this man without analyzing the family.

13
00:00:52,418 --> 00:00:58,358
where he was born and the impact his grandfather had on him.

14
00:01:05,498 --> 00:01:09,769
READ: Sitting down with the President of the United States is something his father and grandfather

15
00:01:09,869 --> 00:01:11,438
they could never do.

16
00:01:24,751 --> 00:01:31,424
NARRATOR: In charge of the world's fourth largest military, who is Kim Jong-Un?

17
00:01:31,524 --> 00:01:37,463
This is the story of how the younger Kim took control of his country and used the legacy

18
00:01:37,563 --> 00:01:42,168
of his family to position himself on the world stage.

19
00:02:03,222 --> 00:02:10,496
(melancholic music)

20
00:02:10,596 --> 00:02:32,752
(music continues)

21
00:02:32,852 --> 00:02:37,122
The sudden death of Kim Jong-Il, the second man to lead the country

22
00:02:37,223 --> 00:02:39,025
shook the North Korean people.

23
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:45,932
The future of this corrupt state was a global concern.

24
00:02:46,032 --> 00:02:51,104
and became the top priority for the then CIA director.

25
00:02:56,241 --> 00:03:01,613
PETRAEUS: What I remember in particular was the uncertainty

26
00:03:01,713 --> 00:03:05,552
of what Kim Jong-Un would be like as a leader.

27
00:03:07,853 --> 00:03:14,427
I was quite young, under thirty, I had no experience,

28
00:03:14,527 --> 00:03:18,931
He was said to be impulsive and a little stubborn.

29
00:03:20,266 --> 00:03:25,104
What would you do about the nuclear program, the missile program?

30
00:03:26,572 --> 00:03:31,878
So those factors together gave us something to think about,

31
00:03:31,978 --> 00:03:34,447
that's how we felt at that moment.

32
00:03:39,852 --> 00:03:43,923
NARRATOR: North Korea was a unique state, a communist country,

33
00:03:44,023 --> 00:03:46,693
but under the control of a family.

34
00:03:50,763 --> 00:03:56,002
It was founded by Kim Jong-Un's grandfather, who outlived all other dictators.

35
00:03:56,102 --> 00:04:01,240
communists, before handing over his eldest son, Kim Jong-Un's father.

36
00:04:02,941 --> 00:04:08,514
His untimely death at the age of 70 meant that there was little succession planning.

37
00:04:08,614 --> 00:04:11,551
before Kim Jong-Un was brought to power.

38
00:04:17,924 --> 00:04:20,660
And the CIA wanted to know who he was.

39
00:04:26,665 --> 00:04:29,535
PETRAEUS: Material like this is always observed and analyzed.

40
00:04:29,635 --> 00:04:36,075
Experts were working on it trying to find something, but there is no solid basis.

41
00:04:36,175 --> 00:04:41,246
real since trying to do a psychoanalysis of someone like him,

42
00:04:41,346 --> 00:04:47,019
about whom we had so little information, it was practically impossible.

43
00:04:47,119 --> 00:04:50,189
NARRATOR: One reason the intelligence agency's files were lacking

44
00:04:50,289 --> 00:04:55,194
of details was because Kim Jong-Un was not always the obvious successor.

45
00:04:59,098 --> 00:05:05,938
His father had five recognized children, one of whom was a daughter,

46
00:05:06,038 --> 00:05:10,776
but there are no known photographs of her.

47
00:05:10,876 --> 00:05:17,183
Her youngest son was also a woman, but North Korea was not ready for a female leader.

48
00:05:19,118 --> 00:05:22,721
Of the three known sons, Kim Jong-Nam was the eldest,

49
00:05:22,822 --> 00:05:25,325
and at some point it seemed like a good option.

50
00:05:27,093 --> 00:05:29,061
REPORTER: Are you Mr. Kim Jong-Nam?

51
00:05:29,161 --> 00:05:32,998
KIM JONG-NAM: If you're not sure, why are you following me?

52
00:05:33,098 --> 00:05:37,270
NARRATOR: In the early 2000s, he left North Korea for China,

53
00:05:37,370 --> 00:05:39,672
an event that left him out of the discussion.

54
00:05:41,974 --> 00:05:46,379
The next son, Kim Jong-Chul, had no interest in politics,

55
00:05:46,479 --> 00:05:48,814
and preferred to play guitar in a band.

56
00:05:48,915 --> 00:05:50,650
REPORTER: Why are you in London, Mr. Kim?

57
00:05:50,750 --> 00:05:52,685
NARRATOR: He has been a solitary figure ever since.

58
00:05:52,785 --> 00:05:55,354
has been seen abroad at rock concerts.

59
00:05:55,454 --> 00:05:56,889
MAN: Believed to be Kim Jong-Chol.

60
00:05:58,457 --> 00:06:01,193
He is seen smiling while enjoying a concert

61
00:06:01,293 --> 00:06:04,063
by Eric Clapton at the Royal Albert Hall in London.

62
00:06:08,233 --> 00:06:12,171
NARRATOR: Which left Kim Jong-Un for the role.

63
00:06:12,271 --> 00:06:15,374
Third in line for the throne, and the only choice

64
00:06:15,475 --> 00:06:17,643
to continue the family dynasty.

65
00:06:24,383 --> 00:06:28,187
LEE: My first impression was how vulnerable she was.

66
00:06:30,122 --> 00:06:32,992
But I wondered, "Is that pain tinged with fear?"

67
00:06:37,096 --> 00:06:44,136
It is not simply taking the leadership of a rich country

68
00:06:44,236 --> 00:06:49,208
and surrounded by comforts,

69
00:06:49,308 --> 00:06:53,278
It is a country with a food shortage,

70
00:06:53,378 --> 00:06:55,847
there is no electricity,

71
00:06:55,948 --> 00:06:59,785
and is isolated from the outside world

72
00:06:59,886 --> 00:07:02,855
It's not an easy job to take on.

73
00:07:08,227 --> 00:07:14,867
I was the first American journalist to work for the local press in Pyongyang.

74
00:07:14,967 --> 00:07:21,674
I was able to talk to the North Koreans about what they were being told about who that young man was,

75
00:07:21,774 --> 00:07:24,377
at least in terms of propaganda.

76
00:07:30,248 --> 00:07:34,954
We were told that Kim Jong-Un would be very different from his father,

77
00:07:35,054 --> 00:07:40,058
more like his grandfather who was legitimately loved by his people

78
00:07:40,158 --> 00:07:42,795
and was extremely charismatic.

79
00:07:45,564 --> 00:07:49,435
He wanted to show people that he was a human person, like his grandfather.

80
00:07:50,202 --> 00:07:57,242
He would often sit on the floor, put the children on his lap and hug them.

81
00:07:57,342 --> 00:08:02,848
That's a great strategy: molding him in the image of his grandfather.

82
00:08:03,649 --> 00:08:07,052
It turns out he looks a lot like his grandfather, and that helps.

83
00:08:08,086 --> 00:08:11,390
By adding all the accessories like the straw hat,

84
00:08:11,490 --> 00:08:14,326
which is identical to the hat his grandfather used to wear,

85
00:08:14,426 --> 00:08:20,700
and replicate his walk, the North Koreans would remember him.

86
00:08:23,469 --> 00:08:27,105
That's one way Kim Jong-Un builds on his grandfather's legacy.

87
00:08:27,205 --> 00:08:29,575
to legitimize his role as leader.

88
00:08:31,643 --> 00:08:37,282
NARRATOR: Kim Jong-Un was willing to use the divine status of his father and grandfather,

89
00:08:37,382 --> 00:08:40,753
but he wanted to be the god of the 21st century, showing

90
00:08:40,853 --> 00:08:44,624
to the North Korean people a sense of modernity.

91
00:08:49,861 --> 00:08:53,365
READ: His campaign to show that he was a different kind of leader

92
00:08:53,465 --> 00:08:57,569
It also included showing his wife in public.

93
00:08:57,670 --> 00:09:04,309
That was very significant because his father rarely went out in public,

94
00:09:04,409 --> 00:09:08,848
We didn't know who his wife was because he never introduced his family.

95
00:09:09,615 --> 00:09:15,154
Now they saw Kim Jong-Un's wife, who was beautiful and elegant.

96
00:09:15,254 --> 00:09:19,458
That's powerful in a society like this, which encourages

97
00:09:19,558 --> 00:09:22,428
people to show a little more individualism.

98
00:09:24,830 --> 00:09:27,867
NARRATOR: Some of the intelligence agencies attributed this type

99
00:09:27,967 --> 00:09:33,072
of modern opening to Kim's formative years outside North Korea.

100
00:09:37,643 --> 00:09:42,281
As a teenager, he spent nine years at a school in Switzerland.

101
00:09:42,381 --> 00:09:45,051
After Kim Jong-Un became leader,

102
00:09:45,151 --> 00:09:47,687
Media interest was focused on that moment,

103
00:09:47,787 --> 00:09:49,988
and the news agencies quickly interviewed

104
00:09:50,089 --> 00:09:53,125
to his former colleagues to gain any kind of perspective.

105
00:09:55,394 --> 00:10:03,069
MAN: Most of the time he was shy, quiet and a quiet boy,

106
00:10:03,169 --> 00:10:07,306
But when we played basketball, it came out.

107
00:10:07,406 --> 00:10:13,746
He could be very aggressive, but in a positive way, loud and a little foul-mouthed.

108
00:10:16,215 --> 00:10:20,987
NARRATOR: According to his caretakers in Switzerland, he was so obsessed with the Chicago Bulls

109
00:10:21,087 --> 00:10:25,290
who slept with a basketball next to him.

110
00:10:25,390 --> 00:10:28,961
LEE: I think we're all very curious to know how much time influenced

111
00:10:29,061 --> 00:10:31,863
that Kim Jong-Un spent in Switzerland,

112
00:10:31,963 --> 00:10:36,168
because we think that exposed him to more Western ideas.

113
00:10:38,203 --> 00:10:42,140
And very early in his reign we began to see that.

114
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:45,977
(music)

115
00:10:46,078 --> 00:10:50,316
It's interesting. He had his own pop group,

116
00:10:50,416 --> 00:10:53,219
a girl band called Moranbong Aktan.

117
00:10:54,286 --> 00:10:57,289
They were a big hit in Pyongyang at the time.

118
00:10:57,389 --> 00:11:03,028
They wore revealing strapless dresses, shorts, miniskirts

119
00:11:03,128 --> 00:11:05,264
and played electric instruments.

120
00:11:06,432 --> 00:11:12,671
We heard them play the theme song from Rocky, which is a very American movie.

121
00:11:12,771 --> 00:11:17,542
So it was all very fresh, very modern and cutting-edge for North Korea.

122
00:11:17,643 --> 00:11:36,928
(applause)

123
00:11:37,029 --> 00:11:41,100
NARRATOR: Kim kept the same military parades established by his father.

124
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:45,504
and his grandfather, but he used his power to satisfy some

125
00:11:45,604 --> 00:11:48,473
of their own personal interests.

126
00:11:48,573 --> 00:11:52,945
In 2013, he asked one of his heroes to come see him.

127
00:12:03,154 --> 00:12:05,357
RODMAN: My name is Dennis Rodman.

128
00:12:05,457 --> 00:12:12,364
He plays basketball for the Chicago Bulls, Detroit Pistons, and LA Lakers.

129
00:12:12,465 --> 00:12:16,067
I made movies and wrote books.

130
00:12:16,168 --> 00:12:20,772
CNN: It's a great cover and a great headline: "I Should Be Dead By Now."

131
00:12:20,872 --> 00:12:22,241
RODMAN: Whatever I imagined, I already did.

132
00:12:27,412 --> 00:12:29,181
I am that man, Dennis Rodman.

133
00:12:31,484 --> 00:12:33,319
I knew nothing about North Korea.

134
00:12:38,857 --> 00:12:43,829
Something happened in 2013,

135
00:12:43,929 --> 00:12:49,435
The North Korean leader wanted to meet someone from the Chicago Bulls.

136
00:12:49,535 --> 00:12:56,876
And I heard that they had invited Michael Jordan first and he said no, I don't want to go.

137
00:12:56,976 --> 00:13:00,245
So I guess I was the second choice, they said:

138
00:13:00,345 --> 00:13:02,915
"Dennis, would you like to go to North Korea?" I said, "Yeah, sure."

139
00:13:06,018 --> 00:13:09,755
I wasn't worried I thought it was like going to Las Vegas

140
00:13:09,855 --> 00:13:11,089
that would be a crazy trip,

141
00:13:11,189 --> 00:13:14,893
that I would go out with prostitutes, we would go here and there,

142
00:13:14,993 --> 00:13:18,530
we would be in the pool, I thought it would be great, something crazy

143
00:13:18,630 --> 00:13:21,033
I thought, okay, I'll put this trip on my resume.

144
00:13:22,768 --> 00:13:24,970
That's what I thought. I'd go play basketball,

145
00:13:25,070 --> 00:13:28,674
It was all, greet people, take some photos and that was it.

146
00:13:30,576 --> 00:13:32,411
But a big surprise awaited me.

147
00:13:36,781 --> 00:13:46,158
(music)

148
00:13:46,258 --> 00:13:50,829
NARRATOR: Former NBA star Dennis Rodman was about to participate

149
00:13:50,929 --> 00:13:53,632
in the strangest match of his entire career.

150
00:14:03,976 --> 00:14:08,447
RODMAN: I walked into the stadium and then one of the leaders did...

151
00:14:10,382 --> 00:14:15,087
I said, 'What's up?' And he said, 'Why don't you sit over here?'

152
00:14:15,187 --> 00:14:19,058
And I said, "Okay." I didn't know what was going on, I didn't know what was going on.

153
00:14:21,126 --> 00:14:23,761
Then people stood up and started clapping.

154
00:14:23,862 --> 00:14:26,565
(applause)

155
00:14:29,234 --> 00:14:33,239
I thought it was because of me. These people really admire me.

156
00:14:35,441 --> 00:14:39,778
Then I understood, I looked to my left, and thought "is he the one they are applauding for?"

157
00:14:39,878 --> 00:14:41,146
I still didn't know who he was.

158
00:14:41,246 --> 00:15:04,636
(music)

159
00:15:04,736 --> 00:15:06,338
Oh, I remember this.

160
00:15:06,438 --> 00:15:09,608
It was "wow, crazy."

161
00:15:12,310 --> 00:15:17,683
That makes you think, it tells you how powerful this man is.

162
00:15:17,783 --> 00:15:20,653
And this lasted ten or fifteen minutes.

163
00:15:21,987 --> 00:15:23,889
I was doing the same thing as them and I thought

164
00:15:23,989 --> 00:15:25,357
"Who the hell am I applauding for?

165
00:15:25,457 --> 00:15:27,492
I'm an American, I don't know what I'm doing."

166
00:15:27,592 --> 00:15:31,363
So I decided, "You know what, I'm going to join."

167
00:15:36,401 --> 00:15:39,237
I was amazed to see those people crying.

168
00:15:40,872 --> 00:15:45,510
I've seen a lot of strange things in my life, I've met a lot of people, presidents,

169
00:15:45,610 --> 00:15:49,748
I saw many things, but this shocked me.

170
00:15:49,848 --> 00:15:52,918
That's when I began to understand what was happening.

171
00:15:53,018 --> 00:15:55,721
COACH: Team, in threes. One, two, three, yes.

172
00:16:00,592 --> 00:16:04,096
RODMAN: When the game started, he started talking to me.

173
00:16:04,196 --> 00:16:07,899
I didn't know what the hell he was saying. He was speaking Korean.

174
00:16:07,999 --> 00:16:12,871
He kept talking the whole game, he talked the whole game.

175
00:16:12,971 --> 00:16:15,974
I think he's a big fan of American basketball.

176
00:16:16,075 --> 00:16:18,377
and his favorite team is the Chicago Bulls.

177
00:16:20,345 --> 00:16:24,516
We hit it off right away, and we chatted, and we laughed, and he said:

178
00:16:24,616 --> 00:16:26,818
"Whatever you want is yours.

179
00:16:26,918 --> 00:16:33,358
The interpreter said, "Dennis, you are one of the few people who have come to our country

180
00:16:33,459 --> 00:16:39,798
and you are one of the few who have sat next to our leader.

181
00:16:43,502 --> 00:16:45,637
For some reason, he really likes you."

182
00:16:50,742 --> 00:16:56,248
You have been very, very kind to me and my American friends.

183
00:16:58,283 --> 00:17:01,486
Sir, thank you.

184
00:17:01,586 --> 00:17:03,355
We will be friends forever.

185
00:17:07,826 --> 00:17:14,666
Now I was starting to feel more comfortable and I was actually having fun with him,

186
00:17:14,766 --> 00:17:17,001
as if we were friends of many years.

187
00:17:17,101 --> 00:17:20,772
And he said, "Dennis, please, I'd like you to come back sometime.

188
00:17:20,872 --> 00:17:22,341
"I want you to come back whenever you can."

189
00:17:22,441 --> 00:17:26,345
I said, "Okay, I'll come back every three months." And he said, "Yeah, do it."

190
00:17:31,449 --> 00:17:34,219
NEWS: Dennis Rodman is back in North Korea,

191
00:17:34,319 --> 00:17:37,723
and will meet with his friend, dictator Kim Jong-Un.

192
00:17:38,991 --> 00:17:46,098
PRESS: Mr. Rodman is on a private trip and our opinion on North Korea

193
00:17:46,198 --> 00:17:47,366
has not changed.

194
00:17:51,670 --> 00:17:55,073
RODMAN: Hey, I'm not the president, and I'm not a politician.

195
00:17:55,173 --> 00:17:57,876
He's like you, or like him, or like anyone.

196
00:17:57,976 --> 00:18:00,879
She wants to let her hair down and have a good time, that's all.

197
00:18:04,816 --> 00:18:10,288
One day, we are outside smoking cigars, drinking tequila, vodka and I think it was the first

198
00:18:10,389 --> 00:18:13,826
time he told me, you know, I'd like to go to the United States.

199
00:18:16,127 --> 00:18:19,698
He said he wanted to go to the United States to watch a basketball game,

200
00:18:19,798 --> 00:18:21,333
I said to him, "What game do you want to watch?"

201
00:18:21,433 --> 00:18:24,769
He said, "I want to go to Madison Square Garden."

202
00:18:24,869 --> 00:18:28,506
And he said it over and over again, Madison Square Garden."

203
00:18:28,606 --> 00:18:30,909
He did this with his hands and got excited.

204
00:18:31,009 --> 00:18:32,210
I told him: You can do whatever you want.

205
00:18:32,310 --> 00:18:34,779
He replied: "I would love to go but I can't.

206
00:18:34,879 --> 00:18:36,515
"I think a lot of people don't like me."

207
00:18:38,450 --> 00:18:39,952
And I thought, "Wow!"

208
00:18:43,488 --> 00:18:44,589
REPORTER: How was your trip?

209
00:18:44,690 --> 00:18:46,024
RODMAN: It was incredible. REPORTER: Incredible.

210
00:18:46,124 --> 00:18:48,660
Kim, Kim Jong-Un? Tell us about that. How amazing was it?

211
00:18:48,760 --> 00:18:49,827
RODMAN: I have to go through, let me...

212
00:18:49,927 --> 00:18:51,963
(simultaneous dialogue)

